                  <provenance><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_426">Oxford, Oxfordshire. Balliol College
                        (Domus de Balliolo). </orgName><p>‘1, a fly-leaf slightly smaller than the
                        volume, which does not look original, has on the verso the scribbled
                        17th-cent. name Jacobus Stuartus, and Liber Collegii de Baliolo Oxon. Anno
                        Domini mdcliiii. This is probably the date, not of acquisition, but of a
                        return from exile, for the book seems to have been seen at Balliol by
                        Leland; he reports (iii. 61) arts. 7, 8, and 12 together, and it is not very
                        likely that the College should have possessed another volume containing
                        these three opuscula. (It is tempting to conjecture that, like all the other
                        handsome copies of rare philosophical or theological texts in this library,
                        it came from William Gray.) It is not recorded in Thomas James’s catalogue
                        printed in 1600; by 1625–30 when Richard James tried to secure it for Sir
                        Robert Cotton [...], it was in the hands of Henry Jackson, fellow of Corpus
                        Christi College, though still recognized as belonging to Balliol. In the
                        library Donors Book, begun about 1630, it appears among the ‘Libri MSti ex
                        incerta donacione’, not in its alphabetical place but added at the end in
                        the same hand, as though newly recovered.’ (Mynors, Balliol,
                     317)</p></provenance>
               </history>
